        "note_tweet": {
          "entities": {},
          "text": "Miyako talk about how she alway been the type to think, “I wanna do what I wanna do!” & pursue whatever interests her but looking back she realizes that a lot of what she does today also came from opportunities that happened along the way.\n\nShe been drawing illustrations for a long time when someone suggested, “Why don’t you try drawing manga?” So she thought, “Alright, I’ll try drawing manga!” & gave it a shot.\n\nThen after joining Yumemita, she thought, “I have percussion experience so I should apply!” She applied became part of Mewtype, & went, “I’m gonna become Mewtype!”—only to find out that her assigned instrument was piano 😭😂\n\nMiyako had never played piano before joining Yumemita but she was actually interested in it. Even in elementary school, she thought about taking piano lessons & at one point she even looked into roll-up pianos because she thought she might be able to buy one with her allowance.🎹\n\nManga was similar. She hadnt drawn manga before high school but she already loved drawing & manga, so she always thought, “If I could draw manga, I like to try it.”\n\nSo while she originally pursued things because she wanted to draw illustrations & become an artist, once she got there, people would sometimes tell her, “Why don’t you try doing this?” Those chances became the starting point for things she never done before.\n\nThat’s exactly what happened with piano through Yumemita. She was given the chance to play piano & thought, “Okay, let’s do it!” She say opportunities like that really are chances.\n\nIf she been completely on her own, she might never have tried some of these things. She feel that “the way things come together” is probably the best way to describe it, someone happens to tell you, “Why don’t you try this?”\n\nLooking at Fuji Miyako’s life timeline, you might get the impression that she always been moving exactly toward the future she imagined for herself.\n\nBut she say life isnt really like that. She compares it to a journey by ship. Of course, you’re on the ship rowing your oars toward the direction you want to go, putting in the effort yourself.\n\nBut along the way, the wind & the current can carry you in another direction, almost like they’re saying, “How about going this way?”\n\nThat’s how she feels about her life so far—the way those encounters, opportunities & circumstances have shaped her journey and brought her to where she is today."
        },
